{"cuisine/latin-american-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/latin-american-cuisine","n":"Latin American cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Cuisine of a people","intro":[["Latin American cuisine is the typical foods, beverages, and cooking styles common to many of the countries and cultures in Latin America. Latin America is a highly racially, ethnically, and geographically diverse with varying cuisines. Some items typical of Latin American cuisine include maize-based dishes arepas, empanadas, pupusas, tacos, tamales, tortillas and various salsas and other condiments (guacamole, pico de gallo, mole, chimichurri, chili, aji, pebre). Sofrito, a culinary term that originally referred to a specific combination of sautéed or braised aromatics, exists in Latin American cuisine. It refers to a sauce of tomatoes, roasted bell peppers, garlic, onions and herbs. Rice, corn, pasta, bread, plantain, potato, yucca, and beans are also staples in Latin American cuisine."],["Latin American beverages are just as distinct as their foods. Some of the beverages predate colonization. Some popular beverages include coffee, mate, guayusa, hibiscus tea, horchata, chicha, atole, cacao and aguas frescas."],["Latin American desserts are as rich and diverse as the region’s culinary heritage. They often feature tropical fruits, creamy textures, and the sweetness of ingredients like sugar, condensed milk, and caramel. Many desserts reflect a blend of Indigenous, European, and African influences. They include dulce de leche, alfajor, rice pudding, tres leches cake, teja, beijinho, flan, and churros."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"latin-american-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latin_American_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Dishes","count":11,"links":[["Bollo","dish/bollo"],["Buñuelo","dish/bunuelo"],["Cardenas (supermarket)","dish/cardenas-supermarket"],["El Cielo (restaurant)","dish/el-cielo-restaurant"],["Frijoles negros","dish/frijoles-negros"],["La Michoacana Meat Market","dish/la-michoacana-meat-market"],["Mi Pueblo Food Center","dish/mi-pueblo-food-center"],["Quince cheese","dish/quince-cheese"],["Rice and beans","dish/rice-and-beans"],["Tamale","dish/tamale"],["Vallarta Supermarkets","dish/vallarta-supermarkets"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Cassava","ingredient/cassava"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Black turtle bean","ingredient/black-turtle-bean"],["Quince","ingredient/quince"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Beans","ingredient/beans"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Corn husks","ingredient/corn-husks"],["Cornmeal","ingredient/cornmeal"],["Maize","ingredient/maize"],["Masa","ingredient/masa"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/indian-south-africans-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/indian-south-africans-cuisine","n":"Indo-South African food","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Diaspora cuisine in South Africa","intro":[["Indian South Africans are South Africans who descend from indentured labourers and free migrants who arrived from British India during the late 1800s and early 1900s. The majority live in and around the city of Durban, making it one of the largest ethnically Indian-populated cities outside of India."],["As a consequence of the policies of apartheid, Indian (synonymous with Asian) is regarded as a race group in South Africa."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"indian-south-africans-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indian_South_Africans#Cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Durban Curry: So Much of Flavour, People, Places & Secret Recipes","ref":"cookbook/durban-curry-so-much-of-flavour-people-places-secret-recipes","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Erica Platter and Clinton Friedman (eds.), with Devi Sankaree Govender","note":"Durban Indian curries, bunny chow and family recipes from local cooks, with the stories behind them.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780620609814","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/balinese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/balinese-cuisine","n":"Balinese cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Indonesia","intro":[["Balinese cuisine is a cuisine tradition of Balinese people from the volcanic island of Bali. Using a variety of spices, blended with the fresh vegetables, meat and fish. Part of Indonesian cuisine, it demonstrates indigenous traditions, as well as influences from other Indonesian regional cuisine, Chinese and Indian. The island's inhabitants are predominantly Hindu and culinary traditions are somewhat distinct with the rest of Indonesia, with festivals and religious celebrations including many special foods prepared as the offerings for the deities, as well as other dishes consumed communally during the celebrations."],["Rice, the primary grain is almost always consumed as a staple accompanied with vegetables, meat and seafood. Pork, chicken, fruit, vegetables and seafood are widely utilized, however as in most Hindu communities, beef is never or rarely consumed."],["Bali is a popular tourist destination, and the area has many cooking schools with daily courses of Balinese cuisine. Night markets, warungs (food stands), and fruit vendors sell local delicacies. Festivals include ornately prepared foods as part of the celebrations. As a popular tourist area, many westernized foods are also available as well."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"balinese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balinese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Food of Indonesia: Delicious Recipes from Bali, Java and the Spice Islands","ref":"cookbook/the-food-of-indonesia-delicious-recipes-from-bali-java-and-the-spice-islands","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Heinz von Holzen","note":"Tuttle cookbook by a Bali-based chef, strongest on Balinese dishes.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9780804845137","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Plecing kangkung","dish/plecing-kangkung"],["Sate lilit","dish/sate-lilit"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Ipomoea aquatica","ingredient/ipomoea-aquatica"],["Kangkung","ingredient/kangkung"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Sambal","ingredient/sambal"],["Shrimp paste","ingredient/shrimp-paste"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Candlenut","ingredient/candlenut"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Grated coconut","ingredient/grated-coconut"],["Kencur","ingredient/kencur"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pig","ingredient/pig"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"ingredient/manioc":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/manioc","n":"Manioc","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Manihot esculenta, commonly called cassava, manioc, or yuca (among numerous regional names), is a woody shrub of the spurge family, Euphorbiaceae, native to South America, from Brazil, Paraguay and parts of the Andes. Although a perennial plant, cassava is extensively cultivated in tropical and subtropical regions as an annual crop for its edible starchy tuberous root. Cassava is predominantly consumed in boiled form, but substantial quantities are processed to extract cassava starch, called tapioca, which is used for food, animal feed, and industrial purposes. The Brazilian farofa, and the related garri of West Africa, is an edible coarse flour obtained by grating cassava roots, pressing moisture off the obtained grated pulp, and finally drying and roasting it."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-manioc"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manioc"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Brazilian cuisine","cuisine/brazilian-cuisine",[["Arabu","dish/arabu"],["Bobó de camarão","dish/bobo-de-camarao"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]]}]},"ingredient/suet":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/suet","n":"Suet","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 8 dishes across 4 cuisines","intro":[["Suet ( S(Y)OO-it) is the raw, hard fat of beef, lamb, or mutton found around the loins and kidneys."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-suet"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suet"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":4,"groups":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine",[["Clootie dumpling","dish/clootie-dumpling"],["Haggis","dish/haggis"],["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"],["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]],["British cuisine","cuisine/british-cuisine",[["Black pudding","dish/black-pudding"],["Christmas pudding","dish/christmas-pudding"],["Spotted dick","dish/spotted-dick"]]],["Chinese cuisine","cuisine/chinese-cuisi